1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,773
This is not our application's latency. It is a film shoot.

2
00:00:03,773 --> 00:00:12,004
Days remaining. Budget burning down. Union violations. And the one that
costs real money: which actors we are paying to sit in a trailer.

3
00:00:12,004 --> 00:00:18,864
Every other project pointing Grafana at something this week is pointing
it at itself. We pointed it at the production.

4
00:00:22,864 --> 00:00:26,219
A line producer does not read dashboards. They ask questions.

5
00:00:26,219 --> 00:00:33,935
So ask one, in plain English. Gemini answers by querying Grafana live,
over the Model Context Protocol, and shows the query it ran.

6
00:00:33,935 --> 00:00:38,296
Twenty six thousand eight hundred dollars. Read off a metric, not
guessed at.

7
00:00:42,296 --> 00:00:51,925
A screenplay goes in. Gemini, on Vertex AI, reads it into typed scenes:
location, set, interior or exterior, day or night, cast, page count.

8
00:00:51,925 --> 00:00:55,937
Then a constraint solver builds the schedule. Google OR-Tools, CP-SAT.

9
00:00:55,937 --> 00:01:00,752
Twelve hour turnaround holds by construction, not by checking afterwards
and apologising.

10
00:01:04,752 --> 00:01:06,472
Now watch what starts this.

11
00:01:06,472 --> 00:01:14,387
The shoot exports its own metrics to Grafana Cloud. A rule evaluates
them, and it is firing: one shooting day visits four locations.

12
00:01:14,387 --> 00:01:19,204
Each company move costs an hour of shooting light. A day in the van.

13
00:01:19,204 --> 00:01:20,925
Nobody is watching a screen.

14
00:01:20,925 --> 00:01:27,806
The Conflict Sentinel asks Grafana which rules are firing, through the
official Grafana MCP server, and reads that alert back.

15
00:01:27,806 --> 00:01:33,312
This is the direction that matters. Grafana does not receive the result.
Grafana starts the work.

16
00:01:37,312 --> 00:01:44,785
The orchestrator routes it. A root agent with no tools of its own, so
nothing it reports can be a figure it invented.

17
00:01:44,785 --> 00:01:50,309
The replanner does no arithmetic at all. Every number it states comes
from a separate CP-SAT run.

18
00:01:50,309 --> 00:01:53,558
Two options, with real cost deltas between two solved schedules.

19
00:01:53,558 --> 00:02:02,656
And when both come out identical, it says so, instead of dressing one up
as a recommendation. The honest answer was: you do not need the extra
day.

20
00:02:06,656 --> 00:02:09,589
Now the agent tries to commit its own recommendation.

21
00:02:09,589 --> 00:02:09,915
Refused.

22
00:02:09,915 --> 00:02:18,064
Not because we withheld the tool. The governance agent has the commit
tool, is told to use it, and the scheduling service turns it down.

23
00:02:18,064 --> 00:02:22,627
The check lives behind an HTTP boundary where no prompt can argue with
it.

24
00:02:22,627 --> 00:02:26,864
An identity in a request body is a claim. A claim cannot commit.

25
00:02:30,864 --> 00:02:36,525
The Producer approves. New version. New audit entry, naming who proposed
it and who approved it.

26
00:02:36,525 --> 00:02:43,248
Sentinel raised it, an agent proposed it, a human decided. The trail
records all three, and nothing rewrites it.

